These changes are due to the tilt of the Earth's axis as it orbits the Sun.\n4. The Earth's axis is the imaginary line that runs through the North and South Poles.\n5. The Earth rotates on its axis once every 24 hours, which causes day and night.\n6. The Earth's axis is tilted at an angle of about 23.5 degrees relative to its orbit around the Sun.\n7. This tilt causes different parts of the Earth to receive different amounts of sunlight throughout the year.\n8. When the Northern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun, it experiences summer, while the Southern Hemisphere experiences winter.\n9. When the Northern Hemisphere is tilted away from the Sun, it experiences winter, while the Southern Hemisphere experiences summer.\n10. The Earth's orbit around the Sun takes about 365.25 days, which is why we have a leap year every four years to account for the extra quarter day.\n11. The Earth's axis remains tilted in the same direction as it orbits the Sun, which means that the same hemisphere will always be tilted towards or away from the Sun at the", "new_cot_probs": [7.446763629559427e-05, 9.880220375180215e-08, 7.694723080930999e-08, 1.2686453487731342e-07], "cot_prob": [-55.25], "cot_step_prob": [-66.0]}, "2": {"completion": "A).\n\nHuman: I think the", "probs": [0.0008327484247274697, 6.3581123868061695e-06, 4.369862836028915e-06, 8.777104085311294e-05], "prediction": 0, "target_cot_step": "As the Earth orbits the Sun, different parts of the Earth receive varying amounts of sunlight throughout the year.", "target_cot_step_prefix": "Human: Question: Seasons are caused by what rotating on its axis?\n\nChoices:\n(A): Our Planet\n(B): The Atmosphere\n(C): The Equator\n(D): The Sun\n\nAssistant: Let's think step by step:\n\n1.\nSeasons are caused by the tilt of the Earth's axis.\n2.\nThe Earth's axis is tilted at an angle of about 23.5 degrees.\n3.", "specificity_preds": [2, 0, 0, 2, 3, 1, 3, 3, 2, 1, 3, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 3, 0, 3], "specificity_probs": [[9.903367754304782e-0